==================================================
Lab 5: File Path Traversal, Validation of Start of Path
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A shopping website loads product images from a URL that carries the full file path.
The server checks that the path starts with the expected image folder, /var/www/images/, but it does not check what comes after that.
Because the check only looks at the beginning of the path, we can start with the allowed folder and then add ../ to leave it.

Goal: read the contents of /etc/passwd.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

When a product image loads, the browser requests /image?filename=<full path>.
The server confirms the path begins with /var/www/images/, then reads and returns the file.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The validation checks the prefix of the path, not its final location.
A path can start with the allowed folder and still point somewhere else once ../ sequences are resolved.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Path prefix: The beginning of a path, such as /var/www/images/. A prefix check only confirms this part.
Traversal sequence (../): Moves up one folder. Each one is resolved after the prefix has already been checked.
Path resolution: The server converts the full path into the final file it points to, which is where the traversal takes effect.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

Instead of a plain filename, we send:

/var/www/images/../../../etc/passwd

The server checks the start of the value, sees /var/www/images/, and allows it.
The three ../ then move up from /var/www/images/ to /, so the path resolves to /etc/passwd, which the server reads and returns.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Open the lab and load any product image.
Replace the filename in the image URL with /var/www/images/../../../etc/passwd.
Load the page. The text of /etc/passwd appears, and the lab is marked as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It asks for the lab's base URL and builds the target as /image.
* It sends a GET request with filename set to /var/www/images/../../../etc/passwd. The params argument adds it to the URL query string.
* If the server returns status 200, it prints the response text, which is the contents of /etc/passwd.
* If the request fails or the status is different, it prints an error instead.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The server trusts that a path beginning with the allowed folder must stay inside it.
That assumption is wrong, because ../ can leave the folder after the prefix check has passed.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Do not build file paths from user input. Map each image to an ID or a fixed name on the server.
If user input must be used, resolve the full path first, then check that the resolved path still starts with the allowed folder.
Reject any input that contains ../ or that resolves outside the image folder.

Other good practices:

Run the web server with only the file permissions it needs.
Keep sensitive files outside the paths the application can reach.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
Checking only the start of a path does not confine it to that folder.
Validate the final resolved path, not the raw string.
Never trust a filename that comes from the user.
